"""Mathematical developments anchored to exact retrieved source lines."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
MONTH = '2026-' + HERE.name
entries = []
def src(stamp, filename='root_receipts.json', record=0):
    raw = json.loads((HERE / filename).read_text())[record]
    return next(line for block in raw['result']['content'] if block.get('type') == 'text' for line in block['text'].splitlines() if stamp in line)
def put(n, title, date, evidence, field, source, receipt, question, summary, definitions, name, statement, proof, setup, calculation, conclusion, limits, extension, references, origin=None):
    entries.append(dict(id=f'Y26-{HERE.name}-{n:03d}', title=title, status='Developed mathematical treatment with explicit hypotheses, derivation and checked example', summary=summary, definitions=definitions, result=dict(name=name, statement=statement, proof=proof), example=dict(setup=setup, calculation=calculation, conclusion=conclusion), limits=limits, extension=extension, references=[dict(label=l,url=u) for l,u in references], original=dict(date=date, month=MONTH, evidence=evidence, field=field, concept=title, origin=origin or 'Recovered dated mathematical starting point; revised and developed here.', question=question, math=statement, finish='Proof or derivation, complete worked example and precise extension', kind='Revised mathematical treatment', refs=[u for l,u in references], source_excerpt=source, retrieval_receipt=receipt, original_question=None, attribution_note='The exact retrieved line supplies the historical evidence and speaker attribution. The revised question, hypotheses, proof and worked example are newly authored; missing original prompts and proofs are not reconstructed.')))
def save():
    (HERE / 'entries.json').write_text(json.dumps(entries, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)+'\n')
    print(f'{MONTH}: {len(entries)} entries')

put(1, 'Hypergeometric face survival gives an exact expected Euler characteristic', '2026-02-03', 'A', 'Random topology', src('2026-02-03T14:48:24Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'How does uniform fixed-size vertex sampling transform the Euler characteristic of a finite complex?',
 'Each simplex survives exactly when all of its vertices are sampled, giving a hypergeometric inclusion probability. Linearity of expectation then yields an exact polynomial transform of the face counts, without pretending that the alternating sum recovers a barcode.',
 ['Let K be a finite abstract simplicial complex on N vertices and f_{b−1} its number of faces with b vertices.', 'Choose an m-element vertex subset S uniformly and let K[S] contain exactly the faces of K whose vertices lie in S.', 'Use ordinary Euler characteristic χ(K[S])=∑_{b≥1}(−1)^{b−1}f_{b−1}(K[S]); the empty complex has χ=0.'],
 'Exact fixed-size sampling identity',
 'Eχ(K[S])=∑_{b≥1}(−1)^{b−1}f_{b−1}·C(m,b)/C(N,b), with the ratio zero for b>m. For independent vertex retention probability p the corresponding formula is ∑(−1)^{b−1}f_{b−1}p^b.',
 'Write the random face count as a sum of indicators over the original faces. A fixed b-vertex face lies in exactly C(N−b,m−b) of the C(N,m) possible samples. Their ratio equals C(m,b)/C(N,b) by factorial cancellation. Taking expectations and the alternating sum proves the identity; independence between faces is unnecessary. Under independent retention, a face’s inclusion probability is p^b instead. For fixed b and m/N=ρ bounded away from zero, expanding the product ∏_{j=0}^{b−1}(m−j)/(N−j) gives log Π=b log ρ−(1−ρ)b(b−1)/(2ρN)+O(N^{−2}); the constant may depend on b and the lower bound on ρ. In dimension three this becomes f₀p−f₁p²+f₂p³−f₃p⁴, with the same hypergeometric replacement for fixed-size sampling. This is an exact finite identity, not an asymptotic density argument.',
 'Take K to be the four-cycle graph, with N=4 and four edges, and sample m=2 vertices.',
 'There are six samples. Four adjacent pairs induce an edge with χ=1; two opposite pairs induce isolated vertices with χ=2. Their mean is (4+4)/6=4/3. The formula gives 4·(2/4)−4·(1/6)=4/3. For m=3 each sample is a three-vertex path and χ=1.',
 'The full finite distribution checks the expectation and reveals the variability hidden by its mean.',
 'The same Euler characteristic can arise from different Betti numbers and different persistence intervals. A real geographic dataset or operational benefit is not established by this identity; the retrieved source explicitly reported no dataset execution.',
 'Derive the variance by classifying pairs of faces according to their union size, using their joint survival probability C(m,|σ∪τ|)/C(N,|σ∪τ|). Determine which additional observations identify Betti curves beyond the Euler projection.',
 [('Herbert Edelsbrunner and John Harer, Persistent Homology—A Survey', 'https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/453/08802')])

put(2, 'Random birth times connect order statistics to an exact barcode law', '2026-02-03', 'A', 'Persistent homology and probability', src('2026-02-03T14:48:24Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'When can the birth distribution of a persistent cycle be derived exactly from its support?',
 'A four-cycle with independent vertex arrival times gives a complete random persistence interval. The example distinguishes a necessary support-survival law from claims about arbitrary cycle detection, where alternative representatives and earlier fillings change the answer.',
 ['Give each vertex of a four-cycle an independent Uniform[0,1] birth time U_i. An edge enters when both its endpoints have arrived.', 'At time one, add one diagonal and the two triangles filling the square. Before that time the filtration contains only the induced cycle graph.', 'Work with homology over a field. Let B=max(U₁,U₂,U₃,U₄).'],
 'A random interval and its expected length',
 'Almost surely the degree-one barcode consists of the single interval [B,1). Its birth cumulative distribution is P(B≤t)=t⁴ for 0≤t≤1, expected length is 1/5, and the expected first Betti number is t⁴ for 0≤t<1.',
 'Before all four vertices arrive, every induced subgraph is a forest and therefore has H₁=0. Once they all arrive, the full cycle is present and has one independent cycle because m−n+1=1. At time one the two triangles and their diagonal form a disk, killing that class. Independence gives P(B≤t)=∏P(U_i≤t)=t⁴. Its density is 4t³, hence E B=∫₀¹4t⁴dt=4/5 and E(1−B)=1/5. Alternatively the layer-cake identity gives expected bar length ∫₀¹P(B≤t)dt=1/5. These two calculations independently connect a random barcode with its expected Betti curve.',
 'Compare observation at t=1/2 with sampling exactly three of the four vertices.',
 'Under independent arrival, the cycle has appeared with probability (1/2)⁴=1/16 at t=.5. Under a uniform three-vertex sample the cycle is never present. For a uniform m-element subset of a larger N-vertex system, a specified b-vertex support survives with probability C(m,b)/C(N,b), agreeing with the separate hypergeometric formula.',
 'A fully declared filtration permits an exact persistence distribution rather than a generic random-barcode slogan.',
 'In a larger complex, a homology class may have several representatives or be filled before all vertices of a chosen support arrive. Support inclusion is then not automatically equivalent to persistence detection.',
 'For a supplied complex, enumerate minimal cycle supports and fillings, use inclusion–exclusion for alternative representatives, and derive a confidence bound for detecting every bar longer than a stated threshold.',
 [('Afra Zomorodian and Gunnar Carlsson, Computing Persistent Homology', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/s00454-004-1146-y')])

put(3, 'Weighted sampling replaces hypergeometric ratios by symmetric polynomials', '2026-02-03', 'A', 'Combinatorial probability', src('2026-02-03T14:48:24Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'How do nonuniform fixed-size sampling weights change face survival probabilities?',
 'A product-weight distribution on subsets has an elementary-symmetric normalizer. This yields an exact inclusion law and a dynamic program for expected Euler characteristic, while clarifying that different without-replacement sampling schemes need not share this law.',
 ['Choose positive vertex weights w₁,…,w_N and a size m. Give an m-element subset S probability ∏_{i∈S}w_i/e_m(w), where e_m(w)=∑_{|S|=m}∏_{i∈S}w_i.', 'For a fixed vertex set F of size b, let w_{Fᶜ} denote the remaining weights and w_F=∏_{i∈F}w_i.', 'This is a specified subset distribution, not an assertion about sequential probability-proportional-to-size draws.'],
 'Exact weighted inclusion and an O(Nm) normalizer',
 'P(F⊂S)=w_F e_{m−b}(w_{Fᶜ})/e_m(w), for b≤m. Consequently expected Euler characteristic is the alternating sum of these probabilities over the faces. The e_r can be computed by E_{j,r}=E_{j−1,r}+w_jE_{j−1,r−1}.',
 'Every subset containing F is uniquely F∪T with T⊂Fᶜ and |T|=m−b. Summing its product weight factors out w_F and leaves the elementary symmetric polynomial in the complement. Dividing by the original normalizer proves the formula. To derive the recurrence, split r-element subsets of the first j vertices into those omitting j and those containing it. Initialize E_{0,0}=1 and E_{0,r}=0 for r>0. Updating r in descending order uses O(m) storage and O(Nm) arithmetic operations. Equal weights give e_m=C(N,m)w^m and recover the uniform hypergeometric probability exactly.',
 'Take weights (1,2,3), m=2, and a graph with only the edge {1,2}.',
 'The three subset weights are 2,3,6, so e₂=11. Vertex inclusion probabilities are 5/11,8/11,9/11 and sum to two, as required by fixed sample size. The edge survives with probability 2/11. Thus Eχ=2−2/11=20/11, also obtained by averaging χ=1,2,2 against probabilities 2/11,3/11,6/11.',
 'The weighted normalizer, all marginals and the topological statistic agree by independent enumeration.',
 'Large or highly unequal weights require stable arithmetic or exact rational computation. The formula alone does not choose scientifically justified weights, guarantee unbiased inference about a population or preserve homology.',
 'Compute pairwise face inclusion probabilities and exact variance using complement symmetric polynomials. Investigate weight design under a fixed budget to maximize detection of a declared family of cycle supports.',
 [('Richard P. Stanley, Enumerative Combinatorics, Volume 2', 'https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511609589')])

put(4, 'Compatibility and gluing can be tested by a linear obstruction', '2026-02-06', 'A', 'Linear algebra and cohomological gluing', src('2026-02-06T11:21:06Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What explicit obstruction distinguishes local consistency from a globally compatible assignment?',
 'Differences prescribed on graph edges glue to vertex potentials exactly when their cycle sums vanish. This finite model gives a complete obstruction theory and clarifies when a rank-sized witness is merely linear redundancy rather than a theorem translating every mathematical branch.',
 ['Let B be the oriented incidence matrix of a connected finite graph. An edge datum a∈R^m is compatible with vertex potentials when a=Bᵀx for some x∈R^n.', 'A cycle vector c satisfies Bc=0. The obstruction functional is cᵀa.', 'For a finite collection of row vectors A_W, define r(W) as the least number of selected rows with the same row-space rank.'],
 'Cycle obstruction criterion and minimal linear witness',
 'The edge data glue if and only if cᵀa=0 for every cycle c. The solution is unique up to an additive constant. Moreover r(W)=rank(A_W), provided individual rows may be chosen freely.',
 'The orthogonal complement of im Bᵀ is ker B, since ⟨Bᵀx,c⟩=⟨x,Bc⟩. In finite-dimensional Euclidean space taking orthogonal complements again proves im Bᵀ=(ker B)⊥, giving necessity and sufficiency. If two potentials solve the equations their difference lies in ker Bᵀ, which consists of constants by connectivity. For r(W), any r selected rows have rank at most r, establishing the lower bound. A basis of the full row space can be selected from its spanning rows by Gaussian elimination, attaining the bound. This proves the source’s witness-size expression in its linear setting; nonlinear or constrained witnesses can behave differently.',
 'Orient a triangle as 1→2→3→1 and prescribe edge increments (2,3,−5).',
 'The unique cycle sum is 2+3−5=0. Taking x₁=0 gives x₂=2 and x₃=5, and the final edge requires x₁−x₃=−5, which holds. Replacing the final datum by −4 gives cycle sum one and no global potential, although each individual edge equation has solutions. The three incidence rows have rank two, so two independent edge equations form a rank witness, but the third still supplies a consistency test on the right-hand side.',
 'Local solvability, coefficient rank and right-hand-side consistency are distinct and explicitly measurable.',
 'The result is a finite linear gluing theorem. It does not establish universal interoperability among proof systems or determine nonlinear obstruction classes without definitions and hypotheses.',
 'Replace scalar potentials by vectors or nonabelian group transports. Identify the appropriate cycle-holonomy obstruction, analyze gauge equivalence and determine which spanning-tree data plus residual loop checks form a complete certificate.',
 [('Allen Hatcher, Algebraic Topology', 'https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATpage.html')])

put(5, 'Prefix geometry has an exact Hausdorff dimension and a carry obstruction', '2026-02-20', 'A', 'Ultrametric geometry', src('2026-02-20T22:33:23Z', 'root_details_2.json'), 'root_details_2.json record 0',
 'How does a symbolic prefix metric differ from Euclidean real-number distance and the p-adic metric?',
 'A weighted symbolic boundary has a provable ultrametric and a computable Hausdorff dimension. Its ordinary radix-value map is continuous but identifies boundary expansions and has no uniform inverse modulus, while p-adic distance compares low-order digits under a different arithmetic convention.',
 ['Let Σ_b={0,…,b−1}^N, b≥2. For distinct sequences let ℓ(x,y) be their common-prefix length and define d(x,y)=a^{ℓ(x,y)}, with 0<a<1; d(x,x)=0.', 'A length-k cylinder fixes the first k digits and has diameter a^k. Use the uniform product measure, giving each cylinder mass b^{−k}.', 'The real radix map is π(x)=∑_{j≥1}x_jb^{−j}.'],
 'Ultrametric, dimension and failure of an inverse Lipschitz estimate',
 'd is an ultrametric and dim_HΣ_b=log b/log(1/a). Sequences agreeing through k digits have |π(x)−π(y)|≤b^{−k}. The map π need not be injective, and small real distance does not force a long common prefix.',
 'Two pairs with prefix lengths r and s force the third pair to agree through min(r,s), proving d(x,z)≤max(d(x,y),d(y,z)). Covering by all b^k cylinders gives Hausdorff s-content at most (ba^s)^k, hence the upper dimension bound. Conversely a ball of radius comparable to a^k has mass at most a constant times b^{−k}=a^{ks₀}, with s₀=log b/log(1/a). The mass-distribution inequality then bounds below the s₀-dimensional Hausdorff content, giving equality. For π, sum the largest possible tail: ∑_{j>k}(b−1)b^{−j}=b^{−k}. The familiar two expansions of a terminating radix value show noninjectivity, so a global inverse modulus is impossible.',
 'Use binary sequences and a=1/4.',
 'The dimension is log2/log4=1/2. The sequences .1000… and .0111… both map to 1/2, despite disagreeing immediately. Replacing the latter by .011…11000… gives distinct real numbers arbitrarily close to 1/2 while their first digits still differ. By contrast, in Z₂ the numbers 1 and 1+2^k are 2^{−k} apart because their low-order digits agree.',
 'Representation geometry and arithmetic topology must be specified before prefix depth is used as a stability invariant.',
 'A real-valued scalar does not become p-adic merely by storing its leading binary digits. Signs, exponents, carries and nonunique expansions need explicit encoding conventions.',
 'For a general decreasing weight sequence w_k, establish dimension under precise regularity assumptions on log w_k, and classify when two weighted prefix metrics are bi-Lipschitz equivalent. Study arithmetic operations using their true moduli of continuity in each representation.',
 [('Kenneth Falconer, Fractal Geometry', 'https://doi.org/10.1002/9781118762868')])

put(6, 'Retaining a rounding residual repairs a concrete cancellation failure', '2026-02-23', 'A', 'Numerical analysis', src('2026-02-23T01:48:31Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'How can a two-component expansion preserve information that ordinary floating-point accumulation loses?',
 'A cancellation example shows exactly why a persistent low component can matter. An error-free sum transform stores the rounded sum and its residual, but neither prefix geometry nor a count-only change guarantees a general optimization benefit.',
 ['Use IEEE-style radix-two round-to-nearest arithmetic with sufficient exponent range and no overflow; a floating value is interpreted as its exact represented real number.', 'For |a|≥|b|, FastTwoSum computes s=fl(a+b), z=fl(s−a), r=fl(b−z). Under its standard rounding assumptions, s+r=a+b exactly.', 'An expansion represents a real number by the unevaluated sum of high and low floating components; collapsing it prematurely loses the recovered residual.'],
 'Exact residual and a cancellation witness',
 'For binary64 a=2^53 and b=1, the rounded sum is s=2^53 and the residual is r=1. Thus subsequently subtracting 2^53 from the represented pair recovers one, while left-associated scalar arithmetic gives zero.',
 'The spacing immediately above 2^53 is two. The exact value 2^53+1 lies halfway between adjacent representable numbers and ties-to-even rounds to 2^53. Consequently z=fl(s−a)=0 and r=fl(1−0)=1, proving the exact decomposition for this witness directly. Subtracting a from the high component then leaves high zero and low one. More generally FastTwoSum’s ordering condition ensures the subtraction extracting the high contribution is exact, and the remaining error is representable; the full error-free-transform theorem is background under the stated rounding model. A persistent low component must be updated through subsequent operations rather than dropped at an arbitrary splice.',
 'Evaluate a three-term sum containing severe cancellation.',
 'Ordinary evaluation fl(fl(2^53+1)−2^53) returns 0, although the real result is 1. Keeping the pair (2^53,1) through cancellation returns 1. Reordering as 2^53+fl(1−2^53) can give a different path, illustrating that a declared accumulation algorithm is part of the numerical method.',
 'The example supplies a reproducible arithmetic mechanism, independent of reported training metrics.',
 'The exact transform depends on floating-point assumptions and does not by itself bound an entire optimizer’s accumulated error. A recorded improvement or failure in historical experiments is not independently reproduced here.',
 'Derive a forward-error bound for a complete high/low update rule, including multiplication, renormalization and subnormal behavior. Compare it with compensated summation at equal cost and test whether the error bound predicts any optimization difference.',
 [('Jonathan Richard Shewchuk, Adaptive Precision Floating-Point Arithmetic', 'https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~quake/robust.html')])

put(7, 'An elliptic curve has nontrivial self-extensions even for a stable line bundle', '2026-02-17', 'A', 'Algebraic geometry and derived categories', src('2026-02-17T09:58:09Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which exact extension calculation distinguishes coherent sheaves on an elliptic curve from a semisimple model?',
 'A stable line bundle on a smooth projective elliptic curve has a one-dimensional self-extension space. Serre duality then explains why splitting claims and vanishing mixed extensions need actual category hypotheses rather than analogy with independent simples.',
 ['Let X be a smooth connected projective complex curve of genus one, and let L be a line bundle. Its canonical bundle is trivial.', 'For locally free L, Ext¹_X(L,L)=H¹(X,End L)=H¹(X,O_X). A line bundle is slope-stable because it has no proper positive-rank subsheaf of smaller rank.', 'A semisimple abelian category is one in which every short exact sequence splits, equivalently all its Ext¹ groups vanish.'],
 'A one-dimensional family of nonsplit self-extensions',
 'Hom(L,L)=C and Ext¹(L,L)=C. Every nonzero class gives a nonsplit exact sequence 0→L→E→L→0. More generally for slope-stable E on X, Ext¹(E,E)≅Hom(E,E)*≅C.',
 'Since End L=O_X and X is connected and projective, global regular functions are constant, giving Hom(L,L)=C. The genus definition gives dim H¹(O_X)=1. For general stable E, any endomorphism is scalar: subtract an eigenvalue and use stability to rule out a nonzero proper image and kernel of compatible slope. Serre duality gives Ext¹(E,E)≅Hom(E,E⊗ω_X)*, and ω_X≅O_X gives the result. A nonzero Ext class is nonsplit by the classification of extensions. Thus the coherent heart cannot be semisimple. This argument concerns an actual curve and actual coherent sheaves; it does not decide whether some separately defined exponential category is semisimple.',
 'Take L=O_X and choose a nonzero class in H¹(O_X).',
 'For 0→O_X→E→O_X→0, the connecting map H⁰(O_X)→H¹(O_X) sends 1 to the chosen class and is therefore an isomorphism. Exactness gives h⁰(E)=1. The split bundle O_X⊕O_X instead has two independent global sections, so the extension’s nonsplitting is visible in a concrete dimension count.',
 'Extension data distinguish categories even when their simple objects or numerical ranks look similar.',
 'The recovered exponential-heart statement does not supply a complete category or vanishing theorem. No classification of all hearts follows from one Ext calculation, and slope-separated objects need not give a semiorthogonal decomposition on an elliptic curve.',
 'For a specified torsion pair in Coh(X), construct its tilted heart and compute extensions between the shifted and unshifted parts. Compare its central charge and Harder–Narasimhan property with a genuinely defined exponential model.',
 [('Tom Bridgeland, Stability Conditions on Triangulated Categories', 'https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2007/166-2/p01')])

put(8, 'Independent shifts classify a semisimple model only under its own hypotheses', '2026-02-17', 'A', 'Triangulated categories', src('2026-02-17T09:53:12Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What is a rigorous bounded-heart classification in a finite semisimple derived category, and which part fails for elliptic sheaves?',
 'The derived category of finitely many independent vector-space simples admits a transparent family of bounded hearts. The proof uses total vanishing of cross morphisms and higher extensions, making clear why it cannot be transferred to an unspecified geometric category.',
 ['Let A=Vec_C^r and D=Dᵇ(A), with simple objects S₁,…,S_r. Every object is a finite direct sum of shifts S_i[n].', 'A bounded t-structure supplies truncations and a heart; use the standard convention that objects of the heart shifted by [1] lie in D^{≤−1}.', 'Morphisms Hom_D(S_i[a],S_j[b]) vanish unless i=j and a=b, in which case they are C.'],
 'Shifted semisimple hearts',
 'Choosing integers n_i gives a bounded heart generated by S_i[n_i], equivalent to Vec_C^r. Every bounded t-structure on this D arises by choosing one cutoff independently on each simple shift-chain.',
 'Each component Dᵇ(Vec_C) is an orthogonal direct factor. In one component, closure of the aisle D^{≤0} under [1] means its set of allowed shifts is upward closed. Boundedness ensures that set is neither empty nor all integers, so it has a least element n. Orthogonality and truncation force the complementary coaisle cutoff, leaving S[n] as the unique simple in the heart. To see that the aisle is determined by its indecomposables, use the finite direct-sum splitting of every object and closure of a t-structure under direct summands. Apply the one-factor argument to each orthogonal component. Conversely the chosen cutoffs define truncations by simply retaining summands on the appropriate side, verifying the axioms directly.',
 'Let r=2 and choose n₁=0,n₂=2.',
 'The heart contains S₁ and S₂[2], and every object is S₁^a⊕S₂[2]^b for nonnegative integers a,b. Cross Hom and every Ext¹ vanish, so exact sequences split. Shifting only S₂ again changes its embedding in D without producing new extension classes. In the elliptic-curve heart, Ext¹(O_X,O_X)=C, so the decomposition into independent orthogonal simple chains is unavailable.',
 'A complete classification is proved for a declared toy category, with the exact obstruction to transferring its proof identified.',
 'The result does not establish semisimplicity or heart classification for the historical exponential category. Noetherian and Artinian properties, mixed Ext vanishing and boundedness must be verified there separately.',
 'Given a nonsemisimple finite algebra, compute its Ext quiver and a torsion pair, perform one explicit tilt, and identify which new heart objects arise as genuine extensions rather than independent shifts.',
 [('Tom Bridgeland, Stability Conditions on Triangulated Categories', 'https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2007/166-2/p01')])

put(9, 'Exponential connections produce periods without proving their independence', '2026-02-16', 'A', 'Differential equations and periods', src('2026-02-16T23:22:57Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which differential connections and integration cycles genuinely produce e−1 and √π?',
 'Elementary rank-one connections give explicit horizontal sections and convergent periods. Their construction separates a valid period identity from an unproved claim of algebraic independence or motivic rigidity.',
 ['For a polynomial Q(z), the rank-one connection ∇=d−dQ on the trivial line bundle has horizontal section e^Q, because d(e^Q)=e^QdQ.', 'Use Q=x on the finite real segment [0,1], and Q=−x² on the whole real line. The latter cycle is noncompact and requires decay.', 'A period identity specifies its integrand, path, endpoints and convergence convention; changing any of them changes the value.'],
 'Two exact exponential periods and a decay sector',
 '∫₀¹e^x dx=e−1 and ∫_R e^{−x²}dx=√π. For complex z=re^{iθ}, |e^{−z²}|=e^{−r²cos2θ}, which decays at least e^{−r²/√2} on |θ|≤π/8.',
 'The first identity follows from the antiderivative e^x. To evaluate the Gaussian, square its positive integral, apply Tonelli’s theorem and switch to polar coordinates: I²=∫_{R²}e^{−x²−y²}dxdy=2π∫₀^∞re^{−r²}dr=π. Positivity gives I=√π. The sector bound follows from cos2θ≥cos(π/4)=1/√2. The recovered polynomial x²−x can also be handled exactly: completing the square gives x²−x=(x−1/2)²−1/4, hence ∫_R e^{−(x²−x)}dx=e^{1/4}√π. Its associated horizontal connection must therefore use Q=−x²+x, not the opposite sign, if decay on the real axis is intended.',
 'Compare the segment and real-line periods with a shifted Gaussian.',
 'The values are approximately 1.7182818, 1.7724539 and 2.2758758 respectively. The shifted Gaussian is finite because its completed square is negative at infinity. The integral of e^{x²−x} on R diverges, despite using the same polynomial with the other sign.',
 'An exact connection and cycle justify the value and its convergence; they do not supply transcendence relations among different values.',
 'No theorem here proves algebraic independence of e−1 and √π, nor the historical exponential-motivic rigidity program. A visual frame can illustrate the connection but cannot replace an independence proof.',
 'Specify a family of exponential integrals, derive its parameter differential equation and rapid-decay cycles, and compute connection matrices across decay sectors. State any proposed algebraic-independence theorem separately with its exact field and known status.',
 [('NIST DLMF, Gaussian and Error-Function Integral Representations', 'https://dlmf.nist.gov/7.7')])

put(10, 'Public depth recursions certify algebraic consistency, not sequential work', '2026-02-08', 'U', 'Recurrences and proof composition', src('2026-02-08T04:05:05Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What does the depth law c_{d+1}=c_d² prove, and what extra hypothesis is needed to certify an execution history?',
 'The double-exponential recurrence is solved exactly and linearized by a double logarithm. An elementary bad-event union bound explains a conditional composition claim, while showing that public endpoint consistency alone does not certify how an endpoint was computed.',
 ['Fix c₀>1 and integer d≥0, with c_{d+1}=c_d². Let a claimed trajectory contain both its endpoints and a declared local policy.', 'A verifier is algebraically sound with error α if an accepted false algebraic statement occurs with probability at most α under its defined experiment.', 'A second verifier is sound with error β for its own execution statement; a composed verifier accepts only when both accept.'],
 'Closed form and conditional composition bound',
 'c_d=c₀^{2^d} and log log c_d=d log2+log log c₀. If every invalid composed claim implies failure of at least one of the two stated soundness properties, then its acceptance probability is at most α+β.',
 'Induction proves c_d=c₀^{2^d}; taking two logarithms gives the affine coordinate. For the probability statement let E₁,E₂ be the two bad-acceptance events. The hypothesis says invalid acceptance is contained in E₁∪E₂, so the union bound gives P≤P(E₁)+P(E₂)≤α+β, without assuming independence. The implication must actually be established for the composed statement; using a security parameter label does not establish it. The placeholder identity (c g)/(g/c)=c² is valid for nonzero g and c, but cancellation removes all dependence on g, including whether g is prime.',
 'Set c₀=313 and d=0,1,2.',
 'The first values are 313,97969,9597924961. The double-log increments are exactly log2. A program can compute the endpoint by exponentiation without reproducing a prescribed intermediate history. Also pitch octave equivalence is multiplication f↦2f; squaring f↦f² is not that same transformation unless an additional coordinate convention is chosen.',
 'Endpoint arithmetic, depth coordinates, music analogies and execution claims are separately defined and checkable.',
 'Public deterministic SDS is not a secrecy or hardness assumption. A Lipschitz policy or total-variation rule must be verified on supplied intermediate states; endpoint mean consistency alone does not force a unique path.',
 'Define an explicit trajectory language, prove which local constraints are committed and checked, and reduce invalid accepted histories to a precisely stated proof-system soundness event. Keep sequentiality assumptions distinct from the elementary recurrence theorem.',
 [('Dan Boneh, Joseph Bonneau, Benedikt Bünz and Ben Fisch, Verifiable Delay Functions', 'https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/601')])

put(11, 'Hash chains preserve order while holonomy uses a different algebra', '2026-02-14', 'A', 'Finite invariants and noncommutative transport', src('2026-02-14T08:28:44Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'How does an ordered endpoint commitment differ from group-valued path transport, and why is neither automatically a complete history invariant?',
 'A nested hash update is order-sensitive rather than inherently abelian. Matrix transport gives a separate noncommutative loop invariant, and both constructions require declared equivalences and collision assumptions before they can certify histories.',
 ['A hash-chain update has h_{t+1}=H(h_t∥m_t), using unambiguous serialization. Its finite output is an endpoint summary of an ordered sequence.', 'For invertible edge transports U_e, path transport is their ordered product, and gauge changes conjugate loop holonomy at its basepoint.', 'The toy arithmetic hash H(h,m)=3h+m mod101 is only an exact counterexample to order-insensitivity, not a cryptographic primitive.'],
 'Order sensitivity and explicit nontrivial loop transport',
 'Starting at zero, the toy chains (1,2) and (2,1) end at 5 and 7. For U=[[1,1],[0,1]] and V=[[1,0],[1,1]], the commutator UVU⁻¹V⁻¹=[[3,−1],[1,0]] is nonidentity. If the recorded path language allows an edge followed by its reverse, holonomy identifies that two-step record with the empty path; the hash map also has collisions on unrestricted histories.',
 'The hash calculation gives H(H(0,1),2)=3·1+2=5 and H(H(0,2),1)=3·2+1=7. Thus nested endpoint accumulation is not an abelian sum. Conversely (0,3) and (1,0) both end at three for this toy hash, illustrating collisions. Any fixed b-bit hash has only 2^b endpoints, so arbitrarily many finite histories force collisions by pigeonhole; computational collision resistance is a different property. Direct multiplication of U,V and their inverses proves the commutator formula. When backtracking is allowed as recorded history, UU⁻¹=I gives an explicit endpoint collision with the empty word; no such claim is made for the restricted positive-word monoid. Under a basepoint change of gauge G, a closed path product becomes GHG⁻¹, so its conjugacy class, rather than its raw matrix, is the natural gauge invariant.',
 'Apply the commutator holonomy to e₁.',
 'It sends (1,0) to (3,1), proving nontrivial transport without relying on a plotted angle. Its determinant is one and trace is three; these conjugacy invariants distinguish it from the identity, whose trace is two. A different loop can still have the same trace, so a scalar score remains incomplete.',
 'Order commitment and geometric transport can be bound together, but their algebraic roles must remain explicit.',
 'The toy example does not implement TPM policy, cryptographic security or physical attestation. The historical assertion that PCR extension is abelian is mathematically inaccurate; irreversibility and commutativity are different properties.',
 'Specify canonical encodings, admissible path equivalences and a gauge-invariant holonomy certificate. Prove that the committed record binds the intended matrix data, with cryptographic assumptions stated separately from the matrix identities.',
 [('Allen Hatcher, Algebraic Topology', 'https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATpage.html')])

put(12, 'A power spectrum can fail to identify a finite sequence', '2026-02-11', 'A', 'Fourier analysis and identifiability', src('2026-02-11T21:56:24Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which distinct finite patterns can share every Fourier power value, and what does that imply for a spectral fingerprint?',
 'Two explicit binary patterns have identical cyclic autocorrelation and therefore identical power spectra. This finite certificate shows why spectral similarity alone does not identify a generator or certify a unique process, even when every frequency is observed.',
 ['For a real sequence x on Z/NZ, define X_k=∑_j x_j e^{−2πikj/N} and cyclic autocorrelation A_x(r)=∑_j x_jx_{j+r}.', 'Use binary indicator sequences of sets A and B. Then A_x(r) counts ordered differences of set elements.', 'The discrete Wiener–Khinchin identity is |X_k|²=∑_r A_x(r)e^{2πikr/N}, with the displayed sign convention.'],
 'A complete finite spectral collision',
 'In Z/12Z the sets A={0,1,4,6} and B={0,1,3,7} have identical autocorrelation: value 4 at r=0, value 2 at r=6, and value 1 at every other nonzero r. They are not related by cyclic translation or reflection.',
 'Expand |X_k|²=∑_{i,j}x_ix_j e^{−2πik(i−j)/N}, then group pairs by r=j−i to obtain the identity. Enumerating the twelve ordered nonzero differences of each four-element set gives one occurrence at each residue 1,2,3,4,5,7,8,9,10,11 and two at six. Hence all twelve spectral powers agree exactly. Translation preserves the cyclic gap multiset, as does reflection. The gaps of A are {1,2,3,6}, while those of B are {1,2,4,5}; the sets therefore cannot be related by either symmetry. This supplies a nontrivial collision beyond the familiar shift and reversal ambiguities.',
 'Compare the two indicator sequences and any statistic depending only on |X_k|².',
 'Both have four ones, equal DC power 16 and the same energy four. Their full power vectors, spectral-flatness functions with identical regularization, and any common weighted power summaries coincide. Their ordered local patterns differ, and a time-domain test or suitable higher-order correlation can distinguish them.',
 'A spectral diagnostic is a declared feature map whose fibers can contain genuinely different signals.',
 'This does not refute every THSC legality constraint or learned classifier; those require the actual sequence language. It does refute unconditional uniqueness from a power-spectrum fingerprint alone.',
 'Add bispectral or phase-sensitive observations and determine their remaining equivalence classes. For a supplied dyadic language, prove a separating invariant or enumerate its finite collision classes before interpreting a benchmark score as generator identification.',
 [('Norbert Wiener, Generalized Harmonic Analysis', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02546511')])

put(13, 'An anisotropic cone has a declared homothety and a link eigenvalue equation', '2026-02-12', 'A', 'Geometric analysis', src('2026-02-12T23:30:42Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which scaling actually preserves an anisotropic cone metric, and what sign belongs in its harmonic indicial equation?',
 'A coordinate calculation determines the correct homothety weights. On an ordinary metric cone, separation of variables derives the radial indicial roots with an explicit Laplacian sign convention, preventing spectral claims from being inferred from an undefined cone analogy.',
 ['Let g=dr²+r²g_Σ+∑_j r^{2α_j}dz_j² for r>0, with a fixed link metric and real α_j.', 'For the ordinary d-dimensional cone omit the z terms and use Δ_Σφ=−λφ, λ≥0. The Laplacian is ∂_r²+(d−1)r⁻¹∂_r+r⁻²Δ_Σ.', 'A homothety D_c must satisfy D_c* g=c²g; the coordinate scaling of z is part of the definition.'],
 'Exact homothety and harmonic radial exponents',
 'D_c(r,y,z_j)=(cr,y,c^{1−α_j}z_j) satisfies D_c* g=c²g. On the ordinary cone, r^sφ is harmonic exactly when s(s+d−2)−λ=0, giving s=−(d−2)/2±√((d−2)²/4+λ).',
 'Under the map, dr² and r²g_Σ gain c². Each vertical term gains c^{2α_j} from r and c^{2(1−α_j)} from dz_j², again c². This fixes the weights without identifying them with a singular-value exponent from another coordinate convention. For harmonic modes differentiate r^s twice and substitute the link eigenfunction: Δ(r^sφ)=r^{s−2}[s(s+d−2)−λ]φ. Setting the bracket to zero proves the roots. If the nonnegative Laplacian −Δ is used instead, its link eigenvalue convention must change consistently; writing a plus λ with the present Δ_Σ convention gives the wrong roots.',
 'On R³ away from zero, the link is S² and degree-ℓ spherical harmonics have λ=ℓ(ℓ+1).',
 'The roots are s=ℓ and s=−ℓ−1. For ℓ=1 these give the harmonic functions rY₁, a linear coordinate, and r^{−2}Y₁, a dipole singularity. With an additional α=2 vertical coordinate, the homothety scales z by c⁻¹ while r grows by c.',
 'Both persistence of a mode and its singularity depend on a derived radial exponent and boundary conditions.',
 'No universal learning spectrum, secretless verification theorem or Delzant integrality follows from a conical chart. The full anisotropic metric has additional radial-volume terms, so the ordinary-cone indicial equation cannot be transferred unchanged.',
 'Compute the full anisotropic Laplacian, specify self-adjoint boundary conditions near r=0 and classify square-integrable radial modes. For torus actions, state the symplectic moment-polytope hypotheses before asking for integral lattice normals.',
 [('Jeff Cheeger, On the Spectral Geometry of Spaces with Cone-like Singularities', 'https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.76.5.2103')])

put(14, 'A secretless feature verifier factors through its observation fibers', '2026-02-13', 'A', 'Identifiability and quotient maps', src('2026-02-13T00:15:40Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What exact hypothesis makes a decision rule depend only on a public feature map?',
 'A function factors through an observation map precisely when it is constant on every observation fiber. This elementary theorem yields a sharp impossibility statement for indistinguishable valid and invalid histories, without asserting that every possible public verifier must use the same features.',
 ['Let X be a set of histories, F:X→Y an observed feature map and V:X→{0,1} a deterministic verifier.', 'Write x∼x′ when F(x)=F(x′), and restrict Y to F(X) when constructing a factorization.', 'A randomized verifier based only on F has an acceptance probability v(F(x)); equal features then imply equal acceptance laws.'],
 'Fiberwise factorization criterion',
 'There exists v:F(X)→{0,1} with V=v∘F if and only if V is constant on every fiber. If one fiber contains both a valid and an invalid history, no verifier based only on F can accept the former with probability at least 1−ε and the latter with probability at most ε for ε<1/2.',
 'Necessity follows by substitution: equal F values give equal v values. For sufficiency define v(y)=V(x) for any preimage x of y; fiberwise constancy proves independence of the choice. This is a set-theoretic factorization, requiring neither conical geometry nor secrecy. In the randomized case let the common acceptance probability be p. The two desired guarantees require p≥1−ε and p≤ε, impossible unless ε≥1/2. Consequently a lower bound requires construction of an actual mixed-validity fiber for the declared observation map, not merely a claim that the system has no secrets.',
 'Let X consist of binary words of length four and F record only the number of ones.',
 'The words 0011 and 0101 both have F=2. If the specification declares adjacent ones valid and separated ones invalid, the fiber contains opposite labels. A count-only verifier cannot distinguish them, whereas a verifier reading the full word can. Likewise the homometric Fourier patterns in the spectral example are indistinguishable to power-only features but distinguishable to their original sequences.',
 'The limitation belongs to a precise observation algebra, rather than to all computation in a public system.',
 'Absence of a secret does not force every verifier to factor through a proposed spectral or holonomy score. Different public data, external anchors and richer observations may refine fibers; their properties need separate proofs.',
 'For a supplied verification task, characterize the coarsest sufficient observation partition. Construct explicit valid/invalid collisions or prove separation, and quantify robustness when feature values are known only within a stated error tolerance.',
 [('David Blackwell, Equivalent Comparisons of Experiments', 'https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177729032')])

put(15, 'A derived inverse-limit obstruction can be computed without a geometric analogy', '2026-02-26', 'A', 'Homological algebra', src('2026-02-26T17:26:48Z', 'root_details_3.json'), 'root_details_3.json record 0',
 'How does the tower Z←×2 Z←×2… produce a nonzero lim¹, and why is that different from finite-depth instability?',
 'The standard product-difference model computes this derived limit as Z₂/Z. Its exact algebra identifies what a proposed geometric realization would need to supply, while separating infinite tower behavior from a finite register experiment.',
 ['For a countable tower A₀←f₀ A₁←f₁… of abelian groups define D:∏A_n→∏A_n by D(a)_n=a_n−f_n(a_{n+1}). Then lim A_n=ker D and lim¹ A_n=coker D.', 'For A_n=Z and f_n=×2, use the 2-adic integers Z₂ and map Ψ(b)=∑_{n≥0}2^n b_n, convergent 2-adically.', 'The Mittag–Leffler condition means that for each fixed level, images from sufficiently high levels eventually stop changing exactly.'],
 'Exact obstruction group',
 'For the doubling tower, lim A_n=0 and lim¹ A_n≅Z₂/Z. Its images at level zero are 2^mZ, so it is not Mittag–Leffler. A finite tower does not reproduce this infinite obstruction.',
 'Compatibility a_n=2a_{n+1} makes a₀ divisible by every power of two, forcing a₀=0 and then every a_n=0. For the cokernel, telescoping gives Ψ(Da)=a₀ in Z₂, so Ψ induces a homomorphism coker D→Z₂/Z. It is surjective because every 2-adic integer has binary digits, which may be used as b_n. If Ψ(b)=k is an ordinary integer, define a₀=k and recursively a_{n+1}=(a_n−b_n)/2. The congruences k≡∑_{j<n}2^j b_j mod2^n ensure every a_n is an integer; the recursion then gives Da=b. This proves injectivity of the induced map. The subgroup of ordinary integers is proper in Z₂, so the quotient is nonzero.',
 'Take b_n=1 for even n and zero for odd n.',
 'Its 2-adic sum is 1+4+16+…=1/(1−4)=−1/3 in Z₂. This is not an ordinary integer, so it represents a nonzero lim¹ class. Every finite truncation of Da=b can still be solved backward after choosing its final a value, showing why finite solvability does not remove the infinite obstruction.',
 'The obstruction is an explicit quotient with a concrete nonzero element, not an inferred geometric phase.',
 'The recovered Heisenberg realization was conditional and its bonding maps were not established. A register with depth at most 64 may exhibit finite discrepancies, but cannot by itself instantiate an infinite nonzero derived limit.',
 'Construct the proposed geometric groups and bonding maps, prove they identify with this tower, and track the displayed class under that identification. For finite truncations, define a separate residual invariant rather than call it lim¹.',
 [('The Stacks Project, Inverse Systems', 'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02MY')])

put(16, 'Contraction yields finite-resolution stabilization only with a compatible observation', '2026-02-28', 'A', 'Dynamical systems and inverse towers', src('2026-02-28T00:13:22Z', 'root_details_3.json'), 'root_details_3.json record 0',
 'When does a contraction guarantee that a finite observation eventually stops changing, and what boundary condition is missing for prefix digits?',
 'A geometric error bound gives an explicit resolution time. Exact digit stabilization additionally requires the fixed point to lie inside an observation cell; convergence in an ordinary metric alone cannot rule out endless crossings of a cell boundary.',
 ['Let R be a q-contraction on a complete metric space, 0<q<1, with fixed point x_*. Then d(R^nx,x_*)≤q^n d(x,x_*).', 'Let O_k be a finite-resolution observation and assume O_k is constant on the open ball B(x_*,δ_k), with δ_k>0.', 'For a binary prefix metric, q=2^{−a} and initial depth ℓ₀ give depth at least ℓ₀+an when the metric contraction and encoding are genuinely compatible.'],
 'Explicit resolution bound and a boundary counterexample',
 'If q^n d(x,x_*)<δ_k, then O_k(R^nx)=O_k(x_*). Thus any integer n>log(d(x,x_*)/δ_k)/log(1/q) suffices. With a non-strict cell convention this becomes the familiar ceiling formula after checking equality cases.',
 'Iterate the contraction inequality and compare its right side with the stated observation radius. The radius assumption then gives exact equality of the finite observations; no limit interchange is needed. For binary depth, taking base-two logarithms of d_n≤2^{−ℓ₀}q^n gives the linear-depth lower bound and the source’s N_lin expression with the appropriate strictness convention. To see the missing hypothesis in Euclidean space, use R(x)=−x/2 and O(x)=1_{x≥0}. The fixed point zero lies on the observation boundary. Starting at any positive x, the iterates alternate sign forever while their distance to zero contracts by one half.',
 'Let q=1/2, initial distance one and δ_k=2^{−k}.',
 'For the open-ball criterion, n=k+1 always suffices, while n=k only reaches the boundary. At k=12 a certified interior observation stabilizes by step13. The sign observation in the preceding counterexample never stabilizes at any step, despite exactly the same numerical contraction factor.',
 'Approximate convergence and exact stabilization become equivalent only after the observation topology is controlled.',
 'Stabilization of one orbit is not automatically the Mittag–Leffler stabilization of all tower images. A proof requires a uniform bound over each declared domain and compatible bonding maps; vanishing lim¹ alone does not imply contraction.',
 'For a concrete truncation tower, prove a uniform image-diameter bound and cell-interior condition at every level. Distinguish monotonicity of every register from an aggregate score, and include periodic-map analysis when the derivative has modulus one or greater.',
 [('Stefan Banach, Sur les opérations dans les ensembles abstraits', 'https://doi.org/10.4064/fm-3-1-133-181')])

put(17, 'Empirical Fisher, Gauss–Newton and Hessian are different matrices', '2026-02-03', 'A', 'Optimization and spectral approximation', src('2026-02-03T03:12:20Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which curvature matrix does a gradient-outer-product routine estimate, and when does a spectral projection guarantee descent?',
 'A scalar squared-loss example separates three commonly conflated matrices exactly. An orthogonal projection has a precise first-order descent identity, while a finite Lanczos approximation needs an independently checked residual before its eigenpairs can be trusted.',
 ['For sample losses ℓ_i(θ), the empirical Fisher is F=n⁻¹∑g_ig_iᵀ, g_i=∇ℓ_i. It is positive semidefinite by construction.', 'For a scalar prediction f_θ and squared loss ℓ=(f_θ−y)²/2, the generalized Gauss–Newton matrix is G=JᵀJ, while the Hessian is G+(f_θ−y)∇²f_θ.', 'Let P be an orthogonal projector, so P=Pᵀ=P². Consider the update θ⁺=θ−ηP∇L(θ).'],
 'Curvature distinction and projected descent',
 'F,G and ∇²ℓ can differ even in one dimension. If L has M-Lipschitz gradient, then L(θ⁺)≤L(θ)−η(1−Mη/2)||P∇L||², so 0<η<2/M gives descent unless the projected gradient vanishes.',
 'Differentiating the squared loss gives g=(f−y)J and ∇²ℓ=JᵀJ+(f−y)∇²f; its outer product is (f−y)²JᵀJ, proving the distinction. For descent apply the smoothness inequality L(θ+v)≤L+gᵀv+(M/2)||v||² to v=−ηPg. Orthogonality gives gᵀPg=||Pg||² and ||v||²=η²||Pg||², yielding the result. If the projection discards the gradient entirely, the update is zero regardless of large retained eigenvalues. For a unit approximate eigenvector v of a symmetric matrix, residual ||Av−λv|| bounds the distance of λ to the true spectrum; expand v in an orthonormal eigenbasis to prove this immediately.',
 'Take f_θ=θ², y=1 and θ=0.',
 'Then J=0 and g=0, so empirical Fisher and GGN are both zero. But the Hessian is 2(3θ²−1)=−2 at zero, revealing negative curvature invisible to either PSD matrix. At θ=1, the exact-fit gradient and empirical Fisher vanish, whereas GGN and Hessian both equal four.',
 'A metrics harness must label its operator and report what its approximation actually measures.',
 'The projected-descent theorem is local smooth optimization, not a guarantee of lower perplexity, faster training or generalization. LoRA parametrization also changes which parameter-space matrix is being studied.',
 'Implement matrix-vector products for each declared curvature operator, verify symmetry and residuals, and compare projected updates at matched compute and learning-rate conditions. State the approximation subspace and retained gradient energy.',
 [('James Martens, New Insights and Perspectives on the Natural Gradient Method', 'https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v21/17-678.html')])

put(18, 'A Smith certificate computes a quotient only after compatibility is imposed', '2026-02-20', 'A', 'Integer linear algebra', src('2026-02-20T17:24:30Z', 'root_details_2.json'), 'root_details_2.json record 0',
 'How can a small integer certificate verify a finite indicator group without pretending to have recovered a crystallographic classification table?',
 'An exact compatibility kernel and induction image produce a quotient with Smith invariants two and four. The example checks the integer maps, basis choice and unimodular transformation explicitly; external symmetry-indicator data remain a separate provenance requirement.',
 ['Let B=[1,−1,0]:Z³→Z enforce compatibility. Its kernel has basis e=(1,1,0), f=(0,0,1).', 'Let A:Z²→Z³ have columns (2,2,0) and (2,2,4). Then BA=0, so im A⊂ker B.', 'In the basis (e,f), the presentation of im A is S=[[2,2],[0,4]]. A Smith certificate requires integer unimodular U,V with USV diagonal and each diagonal entry dividing the next.'],
 'Exact quotient and certificate',
 'ker B/im A≅Z/2⊕Z/4. The certificate U=I, V=[[1,−1],[0,1]] gives USV=diag(2,4), with det U=det V=1.',
 'Solving x₁−x₂=0 shows every compatible vector uniquely equals x₁e+x₃f, so the displayed basis is a genuine integer basis, not merely a rational spanning set. The two image columns have coordinates (2,0) and (2,4), giving S. Subtracting the first column from the second is multiplication by V and yields diag(2,4). Since V is invertible over Z, its image lattice is unchanged; U is the identity basis change. The diagonal relations independently identify the quotient. Its order is eight, agreeing with |det S|. A matrix with the same rational rank but different Smith factors would produce different torsion.',
 'Classify the compatible vector (3,3,7).',
 'Its coordinates are (3,7), so its quotient label is (1 mod2,3 mod4), of order four. Doubling gives (0,2), nonzero; multiplying by four gives zero. Every quotient class has a representative (a,a,b) with a∈{0,1} and b∈{0,1,2,3}, producing eight distinct classes.',
 'The certificate supports an exact finite algebraic conclusion with all integer lattices exposed.',
 'This newly specified lattice is not an asserted SG2 AII indicator table. Representation bundles alone do not determine compatibility, induced atomic bands, anomaly maps or a physical topological phase; the source itself separates those stages.',
 'For a cited representation dataset, construct the integer restriction and induction maps with explicit bases, prove BA=0, compute a kernel basis and Smith witnesses, then compare the resulting quotient with independently sourced classifications.',
 [('Morris Newman, Integral Matrices', 'https://shop.elsevier.com/books/integral-matrices/newman/978-0-12-517850-1')])

put(19, 'A damped mode gives a Floquet multiplier and an exact memory recursion', '2026-02-19', 'A', 'Linear dynamics and Floquet theory', src('2026-02-19T00:04:00Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What are the exact per-cycle decay and input-memory formulas of a damped linear mode?',
 'Solving a damped oscillator produces an explicit multiplier whose magnitude controls memory depth. The sampled driven mode becomes a weighted convolution; the mathematics quantifies attenuation without establishing the performance of a proposed acoustic device.',
 ['Let u″+2γu′+ω₀²u=0 with 0<γ<ω₀, damped frequency ω_d=√(ω₀²−γ²) and Q=ω₀/(2γ).', 'The damped period is T=2π/ω_d. A complex mode sampled every τ has β=e^{(−γ+iω_d)τ}.', 'For input d_k and gain α, use z_{k+1}=βz_k+αd_k.'],
 'Exact cycle attenuation and convolution solution',
 'One damped cycle has |β_T|=exp[−π/(Q√(1−1/(4Q²)))]. The recurrence solves to z_n=β^nz₀+α∑_{j=0}^{n−1}β^{n−1−j}d_j. Attenuation to amplitude 10^{−3} requires n≥log1000/(−log|β|).',
 'The characteristic roots of the oscillator are −γ±iω_d, so amplitude decays as e^{−γt}. Substituting the damped period and γ=ω₀/(2Q) proves the exact expression. The high-Q approximation e^{−π/Q} drops only the explicitly displayed frequency correction. Induct on n to prove the driven recurrence: multiplication by β advances all previous powers and the new input appends the power-zero term. If |β|<1 and |d_k|≤D, then |z_n|≤|β|^n|z₀|+|α|D(1−|β|^n)/(1−|β|), giving a quantitative bounded-input estimate. Negative or near-π phase changes interference, but not the absolute decay factor.',
 'Use Q=400 and one-cycle sampling.',
 'The high-Q factor is e^{−π/400}≈.992177; after five cycles it is about .961491. The 60 dB amplitude threshold, 20log₁₀|z_n/z₀|=−60, gives approximately 879.52 cycles before rounding up. Sampling once per five cycles instead gives about 175.90 sample steps for the same elapsed physical time.',
 'Sampling interval and amplitude-versus-power decibels account for otherwise confusing depth numbers. Integer depth is obtained by rounding the logarithmic threshold upward; changing the sampling interval changes that count but not the physical attenuation time.',
 'The formula assumes a single linear mode with constant damping and no noise floor. Mode coupling, nonlinearities, thermal fluctuations, transducer losses and fabrication tolerances require additional models and measurements.',
 'For a supplied coupled matrix M and sample interval τ, compute A=e^{Mτ}, bound its transient norm including nonnormality, and derive an input-to-output error budget before assigning a reliable memory depth.',
 [('NIST DLMF, Floquet Theory', 'https://dlmf.nist.gov/28.29')])

put(20, 'Eliminating a PT-style coupler yields Hill form with extra harmonics', '2026-02-20', 'A', 'Linear differential equations', src('2026-02-20T00:25:07Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What exact scalar equation follows from a balanced gain–loss coupler, and when is it actually Mathieu’s equation?',
 'A direct elimination derives the scalar Hill coefficient and exposes both the gain derivative and its square. A periodic gain therefore generally creates more harmonics than Mathieu form permits; trace identities also constrain directional discriminant claims.',
 ['Consider a′=γ(t)a+iκb and b′=iκa−γ(t)b, with constant real κ≠0 and real differentiable periodic γ(t).', 'Hill form is y″+Q(t)y=0 with periodic Q. Mathieu form is the special Q(t)=A−2q cos2t, up to a declared phase and period rescaling.', 'The first-order coefficient matrix has trace zero, so its monodromy has determinant one by Liouville’s formula.'],
 'Exact reduction and a directional trace restriction',
 'The first component satisfies a″+[κ²−γ²−γ′]a=0. For γ=g cos2t this coefficient is κ²−g²/2−(g²/2)cos4t+2g sin2t, so it is generally Hill but not Mathieu. For any determinant-one 2×2 monodromy M, tr M⁻¹=tr M.',
 'Differentiate the first equation: a″=γ′a+γa′+iκb′. Substitute b′ and use iκb=a′−γa. The a′ terms cancel, leaving a″=(γ′+γ²−κ²)a. The harmonic expansion follows from cos²2t=(1+cos4t)/2 and γ′=−2g sin2t. The inverse trace identity follows from the explicit adjugate of a 2×2 matrix: when det M=1 its diagonal entries are exchanged under inversion. Thus merely traversing one fixed reciprocal evolution in reverse cannot generate unequal scalar traces; a directional asymmetry needs an explicitly changed dynamics or observable.',
 'Take constant γ=g and κ²>g².',
 'Then a″+Ω²a=0 with Ω=√(κ²−g²). Over period T the scalar monodromy is [[cosΩT,sinΩT/Ω],[−ΩsinΩT,cosΩT]], determinant one and discriminant 2cosΩT. At κ=2,g=1,T=π/√3 it equals −I and has trace −2. At κ=g the limiting scalar equation is a″=0 and monodromy is a shear, so repeated eigenvalue one need not mean identity.',
 'The full coefficient and monodromy are derived before any small-parameter approximation is applied.',
 'The source’s complete original coupling equations were not recovered. This is a newly specified balanced coupler benchmark, not a reconstructed historical model. A claimed directional correction ±cθq requires separate equations and symmetry analysis.',
 'For the actual supplied periodic coefficients, derive the scalar equation without discarding harmonics, compute the determinant-preserving monodromy and compare perturbative traces with a convergent numerical integrator.',
 [('NIST DLMF, Hill’s Equation', 'https://dlmf.nist.gov/28.29')])

put(21, 'The Mathieu discriminant has a negative q² correction near a=0', '2026-02-20', 'A', 'Floquet perturbation theory', src('2026-02-20T00:25:07Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What is the correctly signed second-order discriminant expansion for y″+(a−2q cos2t)y=0?',
 'Fourier perturbation derives the nonresonant characteristic-value correction, which then determines the trace of the π-period monodromy. Its analytic limit at a=0 has a negative q² term, correcting the sign in the retrieved assistant formula.',
 ['Use the Mathieu equation y″+(a−2q cos2t)y=0 and a Bloch expansion y=e^{iνt}∑_{n∈Z}c_n e^{2int}, normalized by c₀=1.', 'For fixed noninteger ν away from resonances, solve the coefficients perturbatively in small q.', 'The π-period monodromy has determinant one and eigenvalues e^{±iπν}; its discriminant is Δ=2cosπν.'],
 'Second-order expansion with its removable limits',
 'a=ν²+q²/[2(ν²−1)]+O(q⁴). Consequently, away from the initial resonant denominators, Δ=2cos(π√a)+πq²sin(π√a)/[2√a(a−1)]+O(q⁴). At a=0 this gives Δ=2−(π²/2)q²+O(q⁴).',
 'Substitution gives [a−(ν+2n)²]c_n=q(c_{n−1}+c_{n+1}). To first order, c₁=q/[ν²−(ν+2)²] and c_{−1}=q/[ν²−(ν−2)²]. Their sum is q/[2(ν²−1)]. The n=0 equation then gives the a expansion. Invert it as ν=√a−q²/[4√a(a−1)]+O(q⁴), and expand 2cosπν to obtain the trace correction. Although this inversion is unsuitable directly at ν=0, the monodromy trace is analytic in (a,q), and the displayed coefficient has a removable limit there: sin(π√a)/√a→π and a−1→−1. The sign is therefore negative. Translation t↦t+π/2 changes q to −q and conjugates monodromy, explaining evenness in q.',
 'Set a=0 and q=.02.',
 'The second-order prediction is Δ≈2−π²(.02)²/2≈1.998026079. Independently integrating the two fundamental initial conditions over [0,π] checks the trace and determinant numerically. The lowest periodic characteristic value a₀(q)=−q²/2+O(q⁴) is consistent: substituting into Δ≈2−π²a−π²q²/2 restores Δ≈2.',
 'Two independent perturbative consistency checks identify the historical sign error. The coefficient’s analytic continuation is a property of the monodromy trace; choosing a single branch of the characteristic exponent is a separate local operation.',
 'A remainder uniform near every resonance requires a separate analytic argument; individual characteristic exponents have branch behavior even though the trace is analytic. Higher-order coefficient values are not certified by the second-order derivation.',
 'Derive fourth-order trace coefficients from the Fourier recurrence or Volterra monodromy series, prove a uniform remainder on a declared parameter box, and compare a structure-preserving numerical certificate with that bound.',
 [('NIST DLMF, Small-q Expansions for Noninteger Mathieu Order', 'https://dlmf.nist.gov/28.15'), ('NIST DLMF, Mathieu Characteristic Equation', 'https://dlmf.nist.gov/28.2')])

put(22, 'Path induction proves a contractible based-path space, not conservativity', '2026-02-20', 'A', 'Type theory', src('2026-02-20T05:59:40Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which precise contractibility statement follows from identity elimination, and why is it smaller than a foundational bi-interpretability theorem?',
 'The based-path total space is contractible by one use of path induction. Its dependent-section consequence explains a valid transport kernel, while distinguishing this internal theorem from a translation preserving all judgments between foundations.',
 ['In intensional type theory let A be a type and a:A. Define P_a=Σ_{x:A}(a=x).', 'A type X is contractible when it has a center c:X and paths c=x for every x:X.', 'Identity elimination J proves a dependent property of paths by handling the reflexivity case. It is an axiom or rule of the chosen theory, not a result imported from a file name.'],
 'Contractibility of the total based-path space',
 'P_a is contractible with center (a,refl_a). For any family E over P_a, evaluation at the center identifies dependent sections with its fiber E(a,refl_a), up to the appropriate homotopies.',
 'Given (x,p):P_a, eliminate p. In the reflexivity case x=a and p=refl, so the required path from the center to (x,p) is reflexivity. J extends this proof to every p, establishing the contraction. Given an element e in the center fiber, transport e along the contraction paths to define a section at every point. Evaluation recovers e at the center, and path induction proves the inverse composite is pointwise equal to the original section; a statement of equality of functions uses function extensionality if the chosen formulation requires it. This makes explicit which rule supplies each step rather than hiding extensionality assumptions.',
 'Interpret the construction in ordinary sets with discrete equality.',
 'P_a has exactly the pair (a,refl), so its cardinality is one and a section is exactly one element of the corresponding fiber. For a two-element base A={0,1} and a=0, no pair with x=1 exists. This elementary model checks the theorem but does not exhibit higher paths or establish a universe-level equivalence of theories.',
 'The internal contractibility claim has a complete proof and a clear semantic model.',
 'Transport definitions, a record named HoTTKernel and a J-based lemma do not prove syntax translation, inverse interpretation, conservativity or equivalence between a requested foundation and Lean. Those require explicit languages, judgments and metatheorems.',
 'Define the source syntax and target interpretation, prove preservation of formation, introduction, elimination and computation rules, and construct inverse translations with stated propositional or judgmental equalities. Keep normalization and conservativity obligations separate.',
 [('The Univalent Foundations Program, Homotopy Type Theory', 'https://homotopytypetheory.org/book/')])

put(23, 'Fractional analog states still have a finite resolution capacity', '2026-02-24', 'U', 'Metric entropy and computation', src('2026-02-24T07:41:10Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'How much distinguishable information can a bounded scalar or prefix register store at a declared precision?',
 'Packing numbers quantify finite distinguishability without assuming binary hardware. A continuum of possible amplitudes becomes a finite number of robust messages once error is bounded, and a symbolic depth-k register has the same elementary information count.',
 ['Let a scalar register take values in [0,1], with readout error at most ε>0. Two encoded messages are reliably distinguishable only if their closed error intervals do not overlap.', 'A δ-separated set has pairwise distances at least δ. Its packing number counts the largest such set in a metric space.', 'A b-ary prefix register observed through k digits has b^k cylinders, each an observational equivalence class.'],
 'Resolution bound for robust messages',
 'If codewords require spacing greater than 2ε, at most 1+⌊1/(2ε)⌋ can fit in [0,1], with strict endpoint adjustments when equality occurs. A b-ary k-digit observation carries at most k log₂b bits of distinct-message information.',
 'Sort M scalar codewords x₁<…<x_M. Summing their adjacent gaps gives 1≥x_M−x₁>(M−1)2ε, yielding the bound with the stated strictness caveat. Conversely a uniform grid with spacing slightly above 2ε constructs nearly this many robust states. For prefixes, there are exactly b choices at each of k positions, so b^k possible observations; injectively labeling messages by them gives M≤b^k and log₂M≤k log₂b. Arithmetic values smaller than one can be encoded, but their fraction size alone does not increase this distinguishability bound.',
 'Suppose ε=.01 and use a grid with spacing .025.',
 'The values 0,.025,…,1 give 41 robust messages because adjacent separation .025 exceeds .02. Their information is log₂41≈5.358 bits. A binary depth-six prefix register has 64 possible observations and six bits, while depth-five has 32. A continuous surface can use another alphabet, but its capacity comparison must retain its actual error and available range.',
 'Resolution, noise and coding make a proposed analog-computing claim mathematically testable.',
 'This bound concerns a bounded scalar with worst-case error. Parallel registers, temporal coding and correlated noise need their own channel model. No new computability class follows merely from using condensation, fractions or nonbinary levels.',
 'Specify a dynamical input-output channel and error distribution, calculate its metric entropy or Shannon capacity under an energy/time constraint, and construct a robust code with a matching achievable rate.',
 [('Claude Shannon, A Mathematical Theory of Communication', 'https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-7305.1948.tb01338.x')])

put(24, 'Spectral flatness is an AM–GM statistic, not a false-acceptance exponent', '2026-02-08', 'A', 'Spectral statistics and uncertainty', src('2026-02-08T03:39:30Z'), 'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What does a spectral-flatness score prove, and what empirical evidence would support a tiny false-acceptance probability?',
 'The score has an exact range and equality condition but does not identify a probability law. A zero-failure binomial confidence calculation quantifies the enormous gap between a claimed security exponent and a modest benchmark.',
 ['For positive powers P₁,…,P_N define SF=(∏P_i)^{1/N}/[(1/N)∑P_i]. If some powers vanish, use the zero-product convention or explicitly specify regularization.', 'A false-acceptance probability p is defined only after a distribution of invalid inputs and a fixed acceptance rule are supplied.', 'For M independent trials under that distribution, the probability of observing zero false accepts is (1−p)^M.'],
 'Flatness bound and an exact zero-failure upper limit',
 '0<SF≤1, with equality exactly when all powers agree. After zero false accepts in M independent trials, the one-sided confidence upper limit at confidence 1−α is p_U=1−α^{1/M}; for small p_U it is approximately −logα/M.',
 'Apply the arithmetic–geometric mean inequality to the positive powers for the score bound; strict concavity of log gives equality only at equal powers. It says nothing about the frequency of adversarial inputs satisfying a chosen band. To obtain the statistical bound, solve (1−p_U)^M=α. For any p>p_U, observing zero events is less likely than α under the binomial experiment. Expanding exp(logα/M) yields the approximation. A worst-case adversarial security theorem is stronger still and cannot be inferred from independent random trials alone.',
 'Compare two spectra and a million-trial experiment.',
 'P=(1,1,1,1) gives SF=1, while P=(1,1,1,16) gives SF=2/(19/4)=8/19≈.421053. With M=10⁶ and α=.05, zero false accepts gives p_U≈2.99573×10⁻⁶. To make this statistical upper limit below 2^{−128} would require roughly (−log .05)2^{128} independent trials, before considering any adversarial mismatch.',
 'A score range and an observed failure rate are different mathematical objects with different supporting evidence.',
 'The historical PQ-128/192/256 labels and flatness bands do not prove their stated FAR exponents. A Hann window changes the statistic’s distribution and correlations; sample independence and the null model need verification.',
 'Specify the invalid-input family, derive a calibrated null distribution for the exact windowed statistic, and prove a worst-case reduction if a cryptographic security claim is intended. Report finite-sample confidence separately from that theorem.',
 [('Clopper and Pearson, The Use of Confidence or Fiducial Limits Illustrated in the Case of the Binomial', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/26.4.404')])

put(25, 'Linear covariance transport can vanish while nonlinear uncertainty remains', '2026-02-02', 'A', 'Probability and nonlinear geometry', src('2026-02-02T23:27:09Z', 'root_final_sweep.json'), 'root_final_sweep.json record 0',
 'When is JΣJᵀ a valid uncertainty approximation, and what happens at a singular derivative?',
 'The exact pushforward law survives where first-order covariance propagation fails. A squared Gaussian variable gives an explicit nonzero uncertainty invisible to its zero Jacobian, clarifying why a conical or singular coordinate map needs more than a linear error bar.',
 ['For a random vector X with law μ and a measurable map f, the exact output law is f_#μ, defined by P(f(X)∈A)=μ(f⁻¹A).', 'Near a deterministic x₀, write X=x₀+εZ with E Z=0 and covariance Σ. For differentiable f, its linear approximation has covariance ε²JΣJᵀ.', 'A higher-order covariance estimate needs moment bounds and control of Taylor remainders, not differentiability alone.'],
 'A complete singular-map example',
 'If X∼N(0,σ²) and Y=X², then E Y=σ² and Var Y=2σ⁴, although f′(0)=0 makes the linear covariance prediction zero. For y>0 its density is e^{−y/(2σ²)}/[√(2π)σ√y].',
 'The Gaussian moments are E X²=σ² and E X⁴=3σ⁴, so the variance is 3σ⁴−σ⁴. For the density, each y>0 has preimages ±√y; summing the two Gaussian densities multiplied by the inverse derivative 1/(2√y) gives the stated formula. Its integrable y^{−1/2} singularity reflects the fold at zero. Away from the singularity, write X=x₀+εZ and Taylor expand X²=x₀²+2x₀εZ+ε²Z²; the linear term correctly gives leading variance 4x₀²ε²Var Z when x₀≠0. At x₀=0 the next order becomes leading instead.',
 'Let σ=.1.',
 'The output has mean .01 and variance .0002, with standard deviation about .014142. The linearized estimate at zero predicts both a constant output zero and zero variance, missing the leading effect. Exact pushforward or a second-order calculation restores it.',
 'A rank-deficient Jacobian identifies where a different asymptotic order is required, rather than where uncertainty disappears.',
 'Coordinate covariance depends on the coordinate chart and noise model. From a metric term r^{2α}dz² one cannot infer Var(z)∼r^{2α} without specifying whether noise is isotropic in coordinates, an orthonormal frame or a pushforward model.',
 'Derive second-order covariance formulas with explicit remainder bounds and fourth-moment assumptions. For a supplied cone map, calculate the full pushforward measure and compare it with Gaussian approximations in a stated probability metric.',
 [('Patrick Billingsley, Probability and Measure', 'https://www.wiley.com/en-us/Probability+and+Measure%2C+Anniversary+Edition-p-9781118122372')])

put(26, 'Quasi-static elimination is a resolvent step with a stability condition', '2026-02-02', 'A', 'Convex optimization', src('2026-02-02T21:09:04Z', 'root_final_sweep.json'), 'root_final_sweep.json record 0',
 'Which minimization problem yields Δx_i=−ηg_i/(1+ηλ_i), and when is the denominator a genuine stability certificate?',
 'A proximal quadratic model gives the source’s update exactly in a Hessian eigenbasis. Positive curvature damps stiff directions, while a vanishing or negative model denominator invalidates the convex minimization interpretation.',
 ['Let η>0 and H be a real symmetric matrix with eigenvalues λ_i. Consider q(δ)=gᵀδ+(1/2)δᵀHδ+||δ||²/(2η).', 'The model is strictly convex precisely when H+η⁻¹I is positive definite, equivalently 1+ηλ_i>0 for every i.', 'In an orthonormal eigenbasis write g_i for the gradient components and δ_i for the update components.'],
 'Exact resolvent update and spectral damping',
 'Under strict convexity, q has unique minimizer δ=−η(I+ηH)⁻¹g, hence δ_i=−ηg_i/(1+ηλ_i). If H≥0, ||δ||≤η||g||, and the minimum value is −(η/2)gᵀ(I+ηH)⁻¹g.',
 'Differentiate q to obtain g+(H+η⁻¹I)δ=0. Positive definiteness makes this system invertible and its stationary point the unique global minimum. Diagonalize H orthogonally to obtain each scalar formula. Completing the square with A=H+η⁻¹I gives q(δ)=.5(δ+A⁻¹g)ᵀA(δ+A⁻¹g)−.5gᵀA⁻¹g, yielding the minimum. For λ_i≥0, every damping factor 1/(1+ηλ_i) lies in (0,1], proving the norm bound. These statements concern the quadratic model; actual objective decrease requires an upper-model or remainder condition.',
 'Take H=diag(0,9), g=(1,1) and η=1.',
 'The minimizer is δ=(−1,−.1), compared with the explicit gradient step (−1,−1). Its model minimum is −.55. The stiff second direction is reduced by a factor of ten. If instead λ₁=−1 at η=1, the first quadratic coefficient vanishes and a nonzero g₁ makes the model unbounded below, so the apparent infinite update signals a failed assumption.',
 'The formula is a checked optimization theorem rather than an automatic mode-elimination principle.',
 'A projected feasible-set flow requires a declared tangent cone, projection and existence theory. The local Hessian model does not establish a global conical normal form or universal learning behavior.',
 'Add a closed convex constraint set and derive the proximal variational inequality. Prove contraction for a strongly monotone operator, then quantify how approximate eigenspaces and inexact solves affect the objective model and its stability margin.',
 [('R. Tyrrell Rockafellar, Monotone Operators and the Proximal Point Algorithm', 'https://doi.org/10.1137/0314056')])

put(27, 'A prime-supported quadratic energy has an elementary constrained minimizer', '2026-02-07', 'A', 'Variational arithmetic', src('2026-02-07T05:05:38Z', 'root_final_sweep.json'), 'root_final_sweep.json record 0',
 'What does a quadratic energy over primes actually determine before asymptotic prime-density or zeta-zero corrections are invoked?',
 'A finite counting measure on primes gives an exact convex minimization problem. Its uniform minimizer and partition function depend only on the number of primes when every site has equal energy, so no fine prime-correlation information can appear in that model.',
 ['Let P_N={p prime:p≤N}, M=|P_N| and dπ_N=∑_{p∈P_N}δ_p. For real values a_p define F(a)=∑a_p².', 'Impose the affine constraint ∑a_p=A. Assume M≥1.', 'For constant site energy c², define Z_N(β)=∑_{p≤N}e^{−βc²}, with a finite fixed N.'],
 'Uniform minimizer and degenerate partition statistics',
 'F(a)≥A²/M, with equality exactly at a_p=A/M for every prime. For constant energy, Z_N=M e^{−βc²}; mean energy is c² and energy variance is zero at every β.',
 'Cauchy–Schwarz gives (∑a_p)²≤M∑a_p², and its equality condition forces all coordinates equal. This proves the unique constrained minimizer; a Lagrange multiplier gives the same result through 2a_p=λ. For the partition function every summand is identical, giving the closed form. Then −∂_βlog Z=c² and ∂²_βlog Z=0. Thus fine spacings or correlations between prime labels cannot influence these statistics, because replacing P_N by any M-element set leaves the model unchanged. Likewise (cp)/(p/c)=c² cancels p whenever c and p are nonzero; prime support remains in the index set rather than in that individual value.',
 'Take N=10, whose primes are 2,3,5,7, and A=8.',
 'The minimizer is (2,2,2,2), with energy 16. The alternative (1,2,2,3) has the same sum but energy 18. For c=2, Z=4e^{−4β}; normalized probabilities are 1/4 each, independently of β, and the Shannon entropy is log4.',
 'The finite measure and optimization are exact and show which information the model retains.',
 'Approximating dπ by dx/log x is an asymptotic statement requiring an error theorem; it is not an equality of measures. Zeta-zero corrections matter only for a quantity that actually depends on prime positions.',
 'Introduce a stated position-dependent energy E(p) or coupling between primes, derive its finite partition derivatives, and use partial summation to identify exactly where a prime-counting remainder enters an asymptotic estimate.',
 [('Tom M. Apostol, Introduction to Analytic Number Theory', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-5579-4')])

put(28, 'Genus-two point counts determine a Frobenius polynomial', '2026-02-04', 'A', 'Arithmetic geometry', src('2026-02-04T04:27:35Z', 'root_final_sweep.json'), 'root_final_sweep.json record 0',
 'How do exact counts over F_p and F_{p²} reconstruct the genus-two local polynomial?',
 'Newton’s identities recover the middle coefficient from two point counts. A completely enumerated smooth hyperelliptic curve over F₃ supplies a certificate for the local polynomial, while global rank and endomorphism claims remain separate questions.',
 ['Let C/F_p be smooth projective of genus two and let N_r=#C(F_{p^r}). Its Frobenius eigenvalues α₁,…,α₄ satisfy N_r=p^r+1−∑α_i^r.', 'Write P_p(X)=∏(X−α_i)=X⁴−a₁X³+a₂X²−pa₁X+p². The reciprocal symmetry and |α_i|=√p are background Weil theorems for curves.', 'Use C:y²=x⁵−x+1 over F₃, with its unique point at infinity.'],
 'Reconstruction and an exact finite-field example',
 'a₁=p+1−N₁ and a₂=[N₂−(p²+1)+a₁²]/2. For the displayed curve N₁=7 and N₂=15, giving P₃(X)=X⁴+3X³+7X²+9X+9.',
 'The first count gives ∑α_i=a₁. Newton’s identity ∑α_i²=a₁²−2a₂ combined with N₂ gives the formula. For smoothness, in F₃ the derivative of f=x⁵−x+1 is 2(x⁴+1). Dividing f by x⁴+1 leaves x+1, and x⁴+1 evaluated at −1 is two, so the gcd is one. An odd-degree squarefree degree-five hyperelliptic equation in odd characteristic has smooth projective genus two with one point at infinity. Enumerate x,y in F₃ and in F₉=F₃[u]/(u²+1), count y²=f(x) and add that infinity point; this gives the stated counts.',
 'Inspect both the count and the Weil-size consistency.',
 'For x=0,1,2 in F₃, f(x)=1, so each x has two y values and N₁=6+1=7. Over F₉ exhaustive enumeration gives 14 affine points and one at infinity. The polynomial factors over R as (X²+aX+3)(X²+bX+3), with a,b=(3±√5)/2. Both |a|,|b|<2√3, so each quadratic has conjugate roots of modulus √3.',
 'The exact point enumeration and independent root-size check support the local arithmetic calculation. The constant coefficient p² and reciprocal linear coefficient encode the genus-two duality symmetry, so checking only the first trace would leave necessary polynomial data undetermined.',
 'This does not prove a global Mordell–Weil rank formula, a Sato–Tate law, BSD or a Jacobian endomorphism classification. Those require global data and additional theorems.',
 'Count over more good primes, compute exact polynomial discriminants and splitting patterns, and compare candidate endomorphism algebras using rigorous exclusion criteria rather than numerical resemblance alone.',
 [('J. S. Milne, Lectures on Étale Cohomology', 'https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/LEC.pdf')])

put(29, 'Swan conductor measures ramification rather than vector-space rank', '2026-02-04', 'A', 'Ramification theory', src('2026-02-04T04:42:25Z', 'root_final_sweep.json'), 'root_final_sweep.json record 0',
 'What does the displayed Swan-conductor sum measure, and why is it not an automatic additive decomposition of arithmetic rank?',
 'A one-dimensional representation with a wild ramification break gives an explicit conductor while its dimension remains one. This separates a valid local invariant from an undefined global rank decomposition and records the exact hypotheses behind the sum.',
 ['Let L/K be a finite Galois extension of local fields, G₀ its inertia group and G_i its lower-numbered ramification groups for i≥1.', 'For a finite-dimensional characteristic-zero representation V, define Sw(V)=∑_{i≥1}|G_i|/|G₀|·codim V^{G_i}.', 'The Artin conductor is codim V^{G₀}+Sw(V). Only finitely many terms are nonzero because the ramification filtration eventually becomes trivial.'],
 'A one-dimensional wild-break calculation',
 'If G₀=G₁=…=G_b=C_p and G_{b+1}=1, and V is a nontrivial one-dimensional character of C_p, then dim V=1, Sw(V)=b and its Artin conductor is b+1. A nontrivial tame character has Swan conductor zero.',
 'For every 1≤i≤b the group acts nontrivially on the one-dimensional space, so V^{G_i}=0 and its codimension is one. The index factor |G_i|/|G₀| is also one. Summing b terms gives b. At i>b the acting group is trivial and the codimension is zero. Inertia invariants are zero, adding one to the Artin conductor. For a tame character the wild groups G_i, i≥1, act trivially, hence every term vanishes even though inertia itself may act nontrivially. Additivity of invariant codimensions under direct sums gives conductor additivity, which is a different statement from an additive formula for a global arithmetic rank.',
 'Use a characteristic-two Artin–Schreier model y²−y=t^{−3} over F₂((t)).',
 'Its standard ramification theorem gives a single lower break b=3 for the nontrivial character. The preceding calculation then yields dimension one, Swan conductor three and Artin conductor four. The break theorem is cited background; the conductor sum itself is explicit once that filtration is specified.',
 'Dimension and ramification can vary independently, so each term in a proposed rank identity needs a separate definition and theorem.',
 'The retrieved λ+κ+ΣSw+δ_tor expression does not define its terms sufficiently to establish a universal rank formula. The example does not claim to prove the general ramification-break theorem from scratch.',
 'For a supplied local representation, compute the ramification filtration and fixed spaces, then compare conductor contributions with an actual global Euler–Poincaré formula or Selmer exact sequence under their full hypotheses.',
 [('Jean-Pierre Serre, Local Fields', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-5673-9')])

put(30, 'Fibonacci transfer matrices give exact word growth and spectral data', '2026-02-12', 'A', 'Symbolic dynamics and transfer matrices', src('2026-02-12T00:54:36Z', 'root_final_sweep.json'), 'root_final_sweep.json record 0',
 'Which counting and substitution statements really follow from the Fibonacci matrix?',
 'The same two-by-two matrix governs a substitution’s letter counts and a finite-state language’s path counts. Its eigenvalues give exact growth in both declared models, while a tight-binding transfer matrix is derived from a separate recurrence rather than identified with them by name.',
 ['Let M=[[1,1],[1,0]]. The substitution H↦HC,C↦H sends its letter-count vector to M times that vector.', 'For binary words forbidding consecutive C symbols, M is also the adjacency matrix on states H,C. A length-L word count is 1ᵀM^{L−1}1 for L≥1.', 'For a Jacobi recurrence t_{n−1}ψ_{n−1}+ε_nψ_n+t_nψ_{n+1}=Eψ_n with t_n≠0, transfer the pair (ψ_n,ψ_{n−1}).'],
 'Exact Fibonacci counts and Jacobi transfer determinant',
 'M²=M+I, its eigenvalues are φ=(1+√5)/2 and −φ⁻¹, and the forbidden-word count is F_{L+2}. The Jacobi transfer is T_n=[[(E−ε_n)/t_n,−t_{n−1}/t_n],[1,0]], with det T_n=t_{n−1}/t_n.',
 'Matrix multiplication proves M²=M+I, yielding its polynomial and roots. A legal word ending in H can follow either state, while one ending in C must follow H; this gives the M recursion. Counts at L=1,2 are two and three, fixing F_{L+2}. Binet’s formula or diagonalization then gives exponential growth rate logφ per symbol. For the Jacobi recurrence solve explicitly for ψ_{n+1}; the displayed transfer and determinant follow. A product over a periodic cell has determinant one when its endpoint hoppings agree, making a Floquet trace meaningful. These are separate concrete uses of transfer matrices, not a theorem that all their spectra coincide.',
 'Take L=4 and four substitution steps from H.',
 'The legal binary-word count is F₆=8; direct enumeration of the sixteen words rejects exactly those containing CC. Substitution counts are M⁴(1,0)=(5,3), with total length eight. Their equal totals at this step do not identify the word language with one deterministic substitution orbit. For uniform hopping one and ε_n=0, T=[[E,−1],[1,0]] has determinant one and trace E.',
 'The construction states the combinatorial object before interpreting its matrix spectrum.',
 'A power-law Fourier exponent is not determined merely by this two-state growth calculation. The recovered sixth-order recurrence needs its initial data and parameter meanings before it specifies a unique sequence.',
 'Construct the substitution dynamical system and its invariant measure, derive autocorrelation recursively, and prove the spectral type of a specified observable. For the sixth-order recurrence, build its companion matrix and classify parameter degeneracies with exact initial conditions.',
 [('Douglas Lind and Brian Marcus, An Introduction to Symbolic Dynamics and Coding', 'https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511626302')])

put(31, 'A rectified accumulator is path-dependent through suffix deficits', '2026-02-10', 'A', 'Reflected dynamics', src('2026-02-10T02:29:46Z', 'root_final_sweep.json'), 'root_final_sweep.json record 0',
 'What exact memory does z_{t+1}=max(0,z_t−s_t) retain, and when does order matter?',
 'The reflected recursion records the largest negative suffix sum of its input. This exact queueing formula explains an endpoint difference between reordered feasible slacks, without calling a noninvertible scalar map a connection holonomy.',
 ['Let z₀=0 and z_{t+1}=[z_t−s_t]_+, where [x]_+=max(0,x) and s_t is a real slack.', 'For n≥1, a suffix sum is ∑_{j=k}^{n−1}s_j with 0≤k<n.', 'The update clips all negative states to zero and is therefore generally noninvertible.'],
 'Closed form and a monotone stability comparison',
 'z_n=max(0,max_{0≤k<n}[−∑_{j=k}^{n−1}s_j]). In particular equal total slack need not give equal endpoints. If every slack is at least δ>0, z_t reaches zero within ⌈z₀/δ⌉ steps and remains zero.',
 'For n=1 the formula is exactly the update. If it holds at n, subtract s_n from each candidate and take the maximum with zero. The old candidates become all suffixes ending at n, including the new one-term suffix from the old zero candidate; this proves the formula by induction. For positive lower-bounded slack, z_{t+1}≤max(0,z_t−δ), which iterates to the stated bound. More generally any cumulative deficit can build a backlog, while later surplus may erase it; clipping makes the order consequential even when the total input sum is fixed.',
 'Compare slack sequences (1,−1) and (−1,1).',
 'Both sum to zero. The first gives states 0→0→1, while the second gives 0→1→0. Their suffix minima are −1 and zero respectively, reproducing the formula. For z₀=3 and every s_t=.4, zero is reached after eight updates. A claimed edge-rider effect can therefore be assessed by exact suffix deficits before introducing a geometric interpretation.',
 'The statistic measures a declared path-dependent accumulation and has an elementary closed-form certificate.',
 'Order dependence alone is not nonabelian holonomy, curvature or a security guarantee. A transport interpretation requires invertible maps or an explicitly defined categorical replacement, together with path composition and gauge rules.',
 'For bounded stochastic slacks derive stationary backlog conditions and tail bounds. For multiple constraints use a vector reflected process, specify its reflection directions and prove when a scalar summary retains enough information to distinguish admissible paths.',
 [('D. V. Lindley, The Theory of Queues with a Single Server', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/mathematical-proceedings-of-the-cambridge-philosophical-society/article/abs/theory-of-queues-with-a-single-server/53E80E039A02BCCC909E5C1D3BBC1AD1')])

put(32, 'A fund target is an annuity recurrence with explicit assumptions', '2026-02-04', 'A', 'Difference equations', src('2026-02-04T21:29:51Z', 'root_final_sweep.json'), 'root_final_sweep.json record 0',
 'What contribution stream would reach a specified nominal target under a constant-return model?',
 'An exact linear recurrence separates contributions, assumed returns and payouts. It turns the historical numerical target into a transparent mathematical scenario, without treating a proposed allocation or future return as established fact.',
 ['Let W_n be a fund balance after year n, W₀=0, r a constant net annual return and c a constant contribution paid at year end. Use W_{n+1}=(1+r)W_n+c.', 'Assume r>−1 and specify that all values are nominal. Fees, inflation and uncertain returns are absent unless introduced explicitly.', 'For a monthly payout D to P recipients, the annual payout is 12DP, a flow with different units from the asset stock.'],
 'Exact target and payout arithmetic',
 'For r≠0, W_n=c[(1+r)^n−1]/r; at r=0 it equals nc. A target T at year n requires c=Tr/[(1+r)^n−1]. A balance by itself does not determine a sustainable payout.',
 'Iterate the recurrence to get c∑_{j=0}^{n−1}(1+r)^j and sum the geometric series. The r→0 limit is n by the derivative of (1+r)^n. If a fixed annual payout b is also made, replace c by c−b in the same recurrence; a nonnegative nominal equilibrium under this simplified accounting needs inflow plus modeled return to cover b. For variable returns, the exact balance is a weighted sum of contributions multiplied by subsequent return products, so order and volatility matter. Neither an expected return nor a portfolio percentage makes the constant-r path certain.',
 'Take T=10 trillion units and n=20.',
 'At r=0 the required yearly contribution is .5 trillion. At the explicitly hypothetical r=.05, it is about .302426 trillion because 1.05²⁰≈2.653298. For P=100 million, monthly D=200 costs .24 trillion annually, while D=800 costs .96 trillion. These are arithmetic flows, not forecasts of affordable distributions.',
 'The target, horizon, contribution timing and payout denominator are all visible in the calculation.',
 'This entry supplies no investment recommendation or prediction. Inflation, taxes, risk, demographics and changes in contributions can materially alter the model; historical policy targets remain proposals.',
 'Replace constant r by a stated stochastic return model, derive the distribution of terminal wealth and shortfall probability, and compare real rather than nominal targets with an explicit inflation process.',
 [('Sheldon M. Ross, Introduction to Probability Models', 'https://www.elsevier.com/books/introduction-to-probability-models/ross/978-0-12-814346-9')])

put(33, 'Changing the scalar domain changes solvability', '2026-02-10', 'A', 'Foundations and elementary number theory', src('2026-02-10T05:00:07Z', 'root_final_sweep.json'), 'root_final_sweep.json record 0',
 'Which hypotheses distinguish solving an equation over integers, rationals, reals or complex numbers?',
 'A linear Diophantine equation and a quadratic illustrate how the chosen scalar domain changes both existence and structure. The domains are connected by embeddings, but their divisibility, completeness and order properties cannot be interchanged.',
 ['Use N={1,2,…} and N₀={0,1,2,…}; this convention makes N⊂N₀⊂Z⊂Q⊂R⊂C.', 'For integers a,b not both zero, gcd(a,b) is their positive greatest common divisor. A Diophantine solution requires integer unknowns.', 'A field allows division by every nonzero element. An ordered field has an order compatible with addition and multiplication; C cannot carry such an order.'],
 'Solvability criteria and a domain boundary',
 'ax+by=c has an integer solution exactly when gcd(a,b) divides c. The equation x²=2 has no rational solution but has two real solutions; x²=−1 has no real solution but has solutions ±i in C.',
 'Every integer linear combination of a,b is divisible by their gcd, proving necessity. Euclid’s algorithm yields integers u,v with au+bv=gcd(a,b); multiply by c/gcd(a,b) for sufficiency. If x²=2 with x=r/s in lowest positive terms, r²=2s² makes r even; writing r=2k makes s even as well, contradicting coprimality. Completeness of R supplies √2, for example as the supremum of positive rationals with square below two. In an ordered field every square is nonnegative; therefore i²=−1 rules out an ordered-field structure on C extending ordinary arithmetic.',
 'Compare 6x+9y=3 with 6x+9y=1.',
 'The first has integer solution (−1,1) because −6+9=3, and all solutions are x=−1+3t,y=1−2t for t∈Z. The second has no integer solution since three does not divide one, but has rational solution (1/6,0). Thus even an elementary equation needs its coefficient and solution domains declared.',
 'Embeddings preserve arithmetic identities while new elements can change solvability and available analytic tools.',
 'This does not classify every equation or foundational system. Complex square-root notation is multivalued unless a branch is chosen, and completeness of R is an additional property rather than a formal consequence of being a field.',
 'For a polynomial or linear system, compare solutions under field extension and integral constraints, compute exact obstruction invariants, and state which analytic arguments require completeness rather than algebra alone.',
 [('Tom M. Apostol, Introduction to Analytic Number Theory', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-5579-4')])

put(34, 'Prefix entropy grows by conditional information, not by metric contraction alone', '2026-02-21', 'A', 'Information on symbolic spaces', src('2026-02-21T21:59:25Z', 'root_details_2.json'), 'root_details_2.json record 0',
 'How does entropy change under prefix refinement, and does a depth-increasing map necessarily create information?',
 'The chain rule bounds the information added by each digit. A deterministic prefix contraction can reduce finite-resolution entropy, while a fresh random prefix increases it, demonstrating that geometric closeness and stochastic information generation require different hypotheses.',
 ['Let X=(X₁,X₂,…) be a random sequence over b symbols and H_k=H(X₁,…,X_k), using natural logarithms.', 'A deterministic prefix map G(x)=0x prepends a fixed digit. In the metric a^{common-prefix length}, it contracts all distances by a.', 'A random prefix map instead prepends an independent uniform symbol U. Its output law includes new randomness not present in the deterministic map.'],
 'Entropy refinement law and two contrasting generators',
 '0≤H_{k+1}−H_k=H(X_{k+1}|X₁,…,X_k)≤log b. For fixed-prefix G, H_k(GX)=H_{k−1}(X). For a fresh independent uniform prefix, H_k(UX)=log b+H_{k−1}(X).',
 'Factor a joint probability into its prefix probability and conditional next-symbol probability inside the logarithm defining Shannon entropy. Summing gives the chain rule. Conditional entropy is a probability-weighted average of entropies on at most b symbols, each between zero and log b; Jensen’s inequality proves the upper bound. For a fixed first digit there is no entropy contribution at that position and the remaining observed coordinates are the first k−1 old ones. For an independent uniform first digit, add its entropy log b by independence. The deterministic map increases pairwise common-prefix length by one, yet its finite observation reveals one fewer old coordinate. Thus the metric refinement inequality alone does not establish uniqueness generation or an entropy source.',
 'Let X be an independent fair binary sequence and k=4.',
 'Originally H₄=4log2. Prepending zero gives H₄=3log2; prepending a fresh fair bit leaves H₄=4log2. If X is deterministic instead, the fresh-prefix operation gives H₄=log2 while the deterministic operation remains zero. These exact counts distinguish information redistribution from genuine randomization.',
 'A proposed generator must specify both its action on sequences and the source of randomness.',
 'Thermodynamic energy costs do not follow from Shannon entropy change without a physical memory model, temperature and permitted operations. Prefix contraction is not an unconditional information-production theorem.',
 'For a declared stochastic transducer, derive its entropy-rate balance and the mutual information retained about the input. Combine it with an explicit channel or thermodynamic model only after identifying discarded, stored and newly randomized degrees of freedom.',
 [('Claude Shannon, A Mathematical Theory of Communication', 'https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-7305.1948.tb01338.x')])

put(35, 'A quadratic Wasserstein proximal step has an exact pushforward solution', '2026-02-26', 'A', 'Optimal transport and variational dynamics', src('2026-02-26T17:18:14Z', 'root_details_3.json'), 'root_details_3.json record 0',
 'Which transport minimization gives the contraction factor (1+λτ)⁻¹, and what does it fail to prove about discrete prefixes?',
 'A quadratic potential on probability measures admits a closed-form proximal map. Completing the square inside a coupling proves contraction exactly, providing a rigorous transport model while preserving the distinction between metric convergence and exact tower stabilization.',
 ['Let μ have finite second moment on R^d, λ>0 and τ>0. Minimize J(ν)=(λ/2)∫|x|²dν+W₂²(ν,μ)/(2τ).', 'W₂² is the infimum of ∫|x−y|² over couplings with x-law ν and y-law μ.', 'Set q=(1+λτ)⁻¹ and T(y)=qy.'],
 'Exact minimizer and contraction',
 'The unique minimizer is ν=T_#μ. For two input measures μ,η, their outputs satisfy W₂(T_#μ,T_#η)=qW₂(μ,η). Repeated proximal steps therefore contract at rate q^n toward δ₀.',
 'Combine the energy and transport cost in one joint integral. Pointwise in y, (λ/2)|x|²+|x−y|²/(2τ) equals (λ+τ⁻¹)|x−qy|²/2 plus λ|y|²/[2(1+λτ)]. The second term is fixed by μ, and the first is nonnegative, vanishing only when x=qy. The deterministic coupling therefore attains the minimum and fixes the unique output law. Scaling any coupling by q gives the upper W₂ bound. Conversely rescale an output coupling by 1/q to recover an input coupling, giving the lower bound and equality. Iteration yields q^n scaling, and W₂(q^n_#μ,δ₀)=q^n(∫|y|²dμ)^{1/2}.',
 'Take λ=2,τ=1/2 and μ=N(2,9) on R.',
 'Then q=1/2, so one step gives N(1,9/4); n steps give N(2^{1−n},9·4^{−n}). Distance to δ₀ starts at √13 and becomes 2^{−n}√13. The contraction and all moments are exact rather than inferred from an empirical fit.',
 'A precise variational problem yields the historical contraction factor with a complete proof. In particular its contraction rate is uniform over all finite-second-moment inputs, rather than fitted to one Gaussian trajectory.',
 'This energy contains no entropy term; adding entropy changes the proximal solution. Metric contraction alone does not prove exact equality of finite prefixes, Mittag–Leffler stabilization or a vanishing derived-limit obstruction without compatible observation hypotheses.',
 'Add relative entropy and derive the corresponding JKO Euler equation, then prove a quantitative convergence theorem under explicit displacement convexity. Combine it with the observation-cell condition from the finite-resolution entry before making a tower-stability claim.',
 [('Richard Jordan, David Kinderlehrer and Felix Otto, The Variational Formulation of the Fokker–Planck Equation', 'https://doi.org/10.1137/S0036141096303359')])
save()
